              "end": 16,
              "start": 15
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The majority held that using a trademark to designate the source of the infringer\u2019s own goods is not a permissible non\u2011commercial use, even if the use is a parody."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Court",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "45a1d9a2e608ac47828291ce48d6a830256f15642804891e3f6a9157452eb597",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-45a1d9a2e608-0002-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 24,
              "start": 24
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The majority also held that the \u201cnon\u2011commercial use\u201d exemption cannot be applied to every parody or humorous commentary."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "45a1d9a2e608ac47828291ce48d6a830256f15642804891e3f6a9157452eb597",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-45a1d9a2e608-0002-0002"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 26,
              "start": 26
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Justice Sotomayor wrote a concurrence noting that some survey respondents mistakenly believe that all parodies require permission from the mark owner."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "45a1d9a2e608ac47828291ce48d6a830256f15642804891e3f6a9157452eb597",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-45a1d9a2e608-0002-0003"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 27,
              "start": 27
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Justice Gorsuch wrote a concurrence emphasizing that lower courts should handle Rogers v. Grimaldi with care."
      }
    ],
